o be a lot of  urgency about the climate uh and then i  think mostly people are just going to  celebrate film and story  could this include an appearance by  president vladimir zelensky given his  background in acting ceremony producer  will packer hasn't ruled it out the  show's still in process um and we're  going to be very thoughtful about how we  acknowledge where we are in the world  but the show's still in process so  that's not something that we would  definitively say one way or another at  this point  at the academy museum it's apparent some  of the most impactful moments in oscar's  history have been those speeches  reflecting the politics of their time  marlon brando won best actor for his  part in the godfather and he chose to  respectfully decline that award and  sense the teen little feather the  treatment of american indians today  by the film industry  excuse me she was cheered and booed  i think people  know that it's a very  well-watched platform where they can  talk about issues which are important to  them climate change is real it is  happening right now  crucially the oscars offers a stage like  no other to have wage equality once and  for all and equal rights for women in  such tumultuous times impossible to  separate the politics from the party  well i'm joined now by la film critics  amy nicholson and brooke obie  we're expecting ukraine to feature  aren't we i was brought up at the sags  the critic choice awards the baftas it's  something that you can't really avoid  this big world event  yeah and and with good reason i'm so  glad i've been to kiev it's one of my  favorite cities on earth and i really am  glad that the world is at least rallying  behind the people of ukraine i mean the  producer will packer he said it is meant  to be a moment of escapism this weekend  but um i mean you can't really avoid  talking about this big political event  it's always going to be on the hearts  and minds of the people that are are  watching but definitely on the  presenters and you know the people who  are going to be accepting their awards  will definitely hear quite a bit about  ukraine i believe and of course the  other big challenge for the academy to  deal with is the fact that the audience  numbers were just so low last year the  worst audience figures ever and they're  trying to change that this year by  bringing back the host we've got wanda  sykes amy schumer regina hall i managed  to catch up with regina and here's what  she had to say to me  it's crazy that you know i'm one of them  but it's exciting it's very exciting and  of course my my my co-hosts are  wonderful and i love them so much we  just get together get high and just go  let's just see where it's going to fall  no we have a great cast of writers and  um they sit and and and we come and  bounce ideas off each other i think  that's what's so great it's so  collaborative  how do we feel about the host coming  back then this is historic i mean this  is the first time that three women will  be hosting the oscars so i think that's  really exciting i think people want to  see  different people hosting they've teased  us a bit with a few sort of trailers  we've got to see the chemistry of them  they seem to to gel quite well don't  they they do although i'll say i'll  stick up for last year's oscars i know  it was much maligned the kind of  quarantined union station one to me it  felt like how the oscars began a group  of people at the ambassador hotel loving  each other's work i like watching the  oscars try new things to be honest  this year's best picture winner then  ladies who do we think is is going to do  it for so long we've been talking about  power of the dog being being the  favorite has things changed now the pga  awards have always kind of predicted  what the best picture winner is going to  be and for the past 22 years and so we  had koda win at the pga awards last week  and so it's definitely going to be at  least a very tight race between coda and  power of the dog what does excite me  though about this year's crop of best  picture nominees is how eclectic they  are i mean i've been sick to the teeth  of how it's usually some historical  drama  this year yes we have that but we've  also got a musical a sci-fi epic a small  family film a giant satire it's at least  trying to push the boundaries of what a  best picture is and i would think we  desperately need that more than anything  it's a great lineup this year well even  if power of the dog doesn't win best  picture we're thinking jane campion  absolutely  absolutely so this is only going to be  the third time in history that a woman  is going to win best director too so  it's absolutely going to jane campion  well she is a phenomenal filmmaker and i  actually managed to catch up with  benedict cumberbatch who told me about  what it was like working with her  it's really all about jane and the  amount of space she allowed for process  the investment she had in it  the allowance of time in prep but also  in production to be introduced as  phil to the crew rather than benediction  you'll meet better to the end he's  really nice this is phil  um  was just really  empowering and enabling i could just  i was it was full permission to be here  which was quite a journey to go on and  so to the films power of the dog is of  course a front runner and there are a  fair few bricks in the running  [Music]  what you're doing getting mixed up with  her jane campion's westin leads the  nominations with 12 including a best  actor nod for benedict cumberbatch 25  years since our first run together 1900  and nothing  it's a long time  british hopes also lie with kenneth  branagh's semi-autobiographical drama  belfast which earned him a director  nomination and dame judy dench a  surprise spot in the supporting actress  shortlist  and the time keeps ticking olivia  coleman and andrew garfield are also  among the british contingent he told sky  news he welcomes oscar recognition as it  shines a light on the work of playwright  jonathan larson  to play jonathan has been one of the  most meaningful  kind of experiences of my life so the  more i get to talk about him and the  more i get to talk about  what he was about ethically and the  songs that he sang and who he was  singing for that feels so yeah it's a  beautiful thing to be able to share his  story  [Music]  but there was no room for spider-man or  bond in the best picture lineup despite  the academy insisting on an expanded  list  it's interesting to see the changes that  they've made in the hope that by having  10 nominees that you'd get some more of  the big hitters in there and the kind of  films that a lot of the public would be  going to see and want to see nominated  at the oscars but very interesting that  no time to die didn't make it into best  picture and only had three nominations  that of course the film that kind of  saved cinema in some ways  the pressure is on the oscars to do  something to halt declining audience  figures with last year suffering more  than a 50 percent drop in viewers  and in a post-pandemic world just as  cinemas relied on films like june with  10 nods and west side story with seven  to bring back customers the academy now  looks to this spielberg magic to lure  audiences back to the oscars  one of our big british hopes this year  is the kenneth branagh's film belfast  could we see sir kenneth maybe getting  screenplay do we think best original  screenplay i'm saying is going to go to  him actually i managed to catch up with  him when belfast came out here's what he  told me about growing up in belfast it  was really to see whether you could find  the story of a family in a difficult  situation where humor and all the other  coping mechanisms we come up with to try  and deal with difficult times could  speak to other people and this lockdown  promoted that i think because the  introspection and the  feeling unsettled that we we've all  shared really drove me back to that time  gothic western the power of the dog is  the favorite to win best pitcher but we  all know academy voters love an underdog  so the question is can surprise indie  hit coda sweep in and take the knights  big prize  [Music]  it is a simple story but koda is one of  the most radical oscar contenders this  year authentically cast and  revolutionary in the insights it offers  into deaf culture  less than a decade ago in the original  french film that it's based on most of  the deaf characters were played by  hearing  actors foreign  in what feels like a landmark moment for  deaf people coder actor troy kotzer is  the first male deaf actor to be  nominated for an oscar marley  but it's been 35 years since marley  matlin's best actress oscar win for  children of a lesser god  within oscar history coda will be  remembered alongside the greats not only  for how deaf people are portrayed on  screen but for how its success  represents an acknowledgement within the  industry that authentic casting matters  but there's still a long way to go  star of a quiet place 2 million simmons  says it's wrong that only a handful of  deaf actors are working in film  i've never done anything without my  family before  regardless of whether koda wins best  picture the success of this boundary  shattering film already represents a win  in terms of who hollywood is now looking  to cast  [Music]  and you can get more oscars intel on our  sky news backstage podcast and don't  forget to watch the ceremony exclusively  on sky cinema from 11 on sunday night  and join me live on sky news on monday  from the vanity fair party with all the  winners  [Music]  [Applause]

